<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eshmunazar II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. 6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eshmunazar II was a Phoenician king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> in the Achaemenid period, probably in the late 6th or early 5th century BCE. He is known from his black stone sarcophagus, found near Sidon in 1855 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>, whose long inscription curses grave robbers and records that the Persian king granted Sidon the coastal towns of Dor and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a>. It is a key source for the Phoenician language.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eshmunazar-ii.jpg" alt="Eshmunazar II" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fred Romero from Paris, France · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eshmunazar_II_sarcophagus_(31289612892).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eshmunazar II was the grandson of Eshmunazar I and the son of King Tabnit I, who ruled for a short time and died before his son was born. Tabnit was succeeded by his sister-wife Amoashtart, who ruled alone until Eshmunazar's birth and then acted as his regent. The family were priests of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> as well as kings: Tabnit's sarcophagus inscription records this for Tabnit and Eshmunazar I, and line 14 of her son's inscription names Amoashtart as a priestess of the goddess. The name means <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a> helps, after the Phoenician god of healing who was the main male divinity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> had passed to Achaemenid rule when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in 539/8 BCE, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a> was then divided into four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> kingdoms: Sidon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and Arwad. In the early Persian period Sidon became the leading city of Phoenicia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> held priestly, military, judicial and diplomatic roles, and building temples was one way of showing royal piety and power. Eshmunazar and his mother built new temples and religious buildings for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baal">Baal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eshmun">Eshmun</a> in several neighbourhoods of Sidon and its adjoining territory. Sidon's naval contribution to the campaign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> was rewarded with Dor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Joppa</a> and the Plain of Sharon.</p><p>The chronology of the dynasty has been much discussed: its inscriptions were long placed in the 5th century BCE, but the French historian Josette Elayi, using coins, seals and inscriptions, placed the reigns of Eshmunazar I's descendants between the middle and the end of the 6th century BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eshmunazar II died at the age of 14, during the reign of his overlord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a>, and was succeeded by his cousin Bodashtart. His sarcophagus is one of only three Ancient Egyptian sarcophagi found outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>; the other two belonged to his parents. It was probably carved in Egypt from amphibolite for a member of the Egyptian elite and taken as booty by the Sidonians during the conquest of Egypt in 525 BCE.</p><p>Treasure-hunters found it on 19 January 1855 in an ancient cemetery south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, outside a rock mound known as Magharet Abloun. Alphonse Durighello, an agent of the French consulate, sold it to Aimé Péretié; it was acquired by Honoré Théodoric d'Albert de Luynes, who gave it to the French state. Its two Phoenician inscriptions, on the lid and in part on the trough, were translated by scholars including Heinrich Ewald, Emil Rödiger and Salomon Munk. It is in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> as AO 4806.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eshmunazar II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Phoenician inscription on the sarcophagus of King <b>Eshmunazar II</b>, discovered in 1855, calls him 'king of the Sidonians' and says his mother was a priestess of Ashtart, 'the goddess of the Sidonians', naming Eshmun and Baal Sidon, 'Lord of Sidon', as the chief gods and giving Ashtart…</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Among the key inscriptions are the sarcophagi of Ahiram, Tabnit and <b>Eshmunazar II</b>, the Bodashtart inscriptions and the Temple of Eshmun, the Kilamuwa Stela, the Karatepe bilingual, the Çineköy inscription, the Nora Stone, the Mdina Steles, the Cippi of Melqart and the Pyrgi Tablets.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>At Sidon the royal family also served the goddess Astarte as priests, as the inscribed sarcophagi of King Tabnit and his son <b>Eshmunazar II</b> record.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Society</a></li></ul></section>
